 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Perpendicular Light:** If $\mathbf{L}$ is perpendicular to $\mathbf{V}$, $\mathbf{V} \cdot \mathbf{L} = 0$, and the adjustment term is zero. No correction is needed [S1, S3, unofficial].
-*   **Non-Linear Depth Cueing:** For complex depth cueing functions, a convolution is necessary for the adjustment term, increasing computational cost [S1, S3, unofficial].
-*   **Gradient Distortion:** Applying depth cueing before shading distorts the gradient vector, leading to incorrect shading. Reversing the order avoids this but requires the adjustment term [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Perpendicular Light:** If $L \perp V$, then $V \cdot L = 0$. The difference term becomes zero, and no adjustment is necessary [S1, S2, unofficial]. This is a common special case to maximize shading effect (side lighting) [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Complex Depth Cueing:** If the depth cueing function is not linear, a convolution is necessary for the adjustment term [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Order of Operations:** Applying depth cueing before shading distorts gradient vectors, leading to incorrect shading if not adjusted [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Combined Depth Cueing and Shading (Reversed Order)
-A technique to combine depth cueing and directional shading by reversing the order of operations (applying depth cueing and interpolation first, then shading) to use a single composite filter $H'$ and avoid two convolutions [S3, unofficial]. It is chosen when computational efficiency is prioritized and the light direction is not perpendicular to the view direction, requiring an adjustment term [S3, unofficial]. The adjustment term is $f(x) d'_{1D}(\mathbf{V} \cdot \mathbf{x}) (\mathbf{V} \cdot \mathbf{L})$, which is zero if $d(x)$ is linear and $\mathbf{L}$ is perpendicular to $\mathbf{V}$ [S3, unofficial].
+### [Combined Depth Cueing and Shading (Reversed Order)](1176-combined-depth-cueing-and-shading-reversed-order.md)
+A variant that explicitly reverses the physical order of operations (depth cueing then shading) to enable a single composite filter application, correcting for the resulting error. It is chosen when computational cost is critical, especially when the light vector is not perpendicular to the view vector.
+
+### [Composite Depth Cueing and Directional Shading](1143-composite-depth-cueing-and-directional-shading.md)
+The parent method that combines linear attenuation and hemispherical illumination. It requires 16 precomputed spectra and is slower due to a high constant factor, though asymptotically less expensive than conventional rendering.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(n^2 \log n)$ for the frequency domain computation, replacing $O(n^3)$ spatial domain computations [S1, unofficial]. The interpolation step is $O(n^2)$ and consumes most of the running time [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the 3D spectrum and intermediate slices [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** A $128^3$ volume can be rendered in a fraction of a second on a conventional workstation [S1, unofficial]. The adaptive filtering scheme reduced the cost of interpolation to about 15% of the non-adaptive case [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Significant speed advantage over conventional volume rendering; no visible difference compared to conventional spatial domain rendering with identical shading and depth cueing [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** The frequency domain projection operator is non-occluding, resulting in a loss of realism [S1, unofficial]. The reversed order requires an adjustment term if $\mathbf{L}$ is not perpendicular to $\mathbf{V}$ [S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Use when high performance is needed for large volumes and occlusion is not required [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When occlusion is critical for realism or when the light direction is complex and non-perpendicular, requiring expensive convolutions for adjustment [S1, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(n^2 \log n)$ for the frequency domain computation, replacing $O(n^3)$ spatial domain computations for shading and depth cueing [S1, S2, unofficial]. The interpolation step, which consumes most of the running time, is $O(n^2)$ [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the 3D spectrum and the volume data. Zero-padding is used (e.g., $106^3$ to $128^3$) [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** A $128^3$ volume can be rendered in a fraction of a second on a conventional workstation [S1, S2, unofficial]. Adaptive filtering can reduce interpolation cost to about 15% of the non-adaptive case [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Significant speed advantage over spatial domain rendering; no visible difference compared to conventional spatial domain rendering when identical shading and depth cueing are applied [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** The frequency domain projection operator is non-occluding, resulting in a loss of realism compared to ray-casting methods [S1, S2, unofficial]. The reversed order approximation may introduce errors if not adjusted, particularly when $V \cdot L \neq 0$ [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When real-time or near-real-time rendering of large volumes is required, and occlusion is not critical.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When accurate occlusion is required, or when the light direction is such that the adjustment term is complex and expensive to compute.
 
 ## Background
-Originated by Takashi Totsuka and Marc Levoy in 1993 [S1, S3, unofficial]. The paper "Frequency Domain Volume Rendering" was presented at SIGGRAPH 1993 [S1, unofficial]. The method replaces $O(n^3)$ spatial domain computations (specifically shading and depth cueing) with $O(n^2)$ frequency domain methods to reduce computational cost [S1, unofficial].
+The method originates from the work of Totsuka and Hudson on Frequency Domain Volume Rendering, presented at SIGGRAPH 1993 [S1, S2, unofficial]. It was developed to address the high computational cost of spatial domain volume rendering techniques, specifically shading and depth cueing, by leveraging the Fourier Projection-Slice Theorem [S1, S2, unofficial]. The technique was demonstrated on CT data of a human skull and 3D scan-converted data of the Utah teapot [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
